      "text": "Let's start by setting the temperature equation equal to 77 degrees and solving for \\( t \\):\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\nRearrange this into a standard quadratic equation form \\( at^2 + bt + c = 0 \\):\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo solve this quadratic equation, we can use the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\nwhere \\( a = -1 \\), \\( b = 12 \\), and \\( c = -27 \\). Substituting these values into the formula gives:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-12 \\pm \\sqrt{12^2 - 4(-1)(-27)}}{2(-1)}\n\\]\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{-2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{-2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-12 \\pm 6}{-2}\n\\]\n\nDepending on which root to choose, we subtract or divide by 6 and then divide by -2. Let's solve both:\n1. \\( t = \\frac{-12 + 6}{-2} = \\frac{-6}{-2} = 3 \\)\n2. \\( t = \\frac{-12 - 6}{-2} = \\frac{-18}{-2} = 9 \\)\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, the correct answer is \\( t = 9 \\).\n\nTo verify, substitute \\( t = 9 \\) back into the original equation:\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 = 77\n\\]\nSimplify this to find \\( T \\):\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the method from our quadratic formula, we expect the larger \\( T \\) value to be 9: \n\nThus, the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees was indeed:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
