      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the largest time \\( t \\) at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77 \\]\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0 \\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we can multiply the entire equation by -1 to make it easier to factor:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNow, we will factor the quadratic equation. We search for two numbers that multiply to 27 and add up to -12. These numbers are -9 and -3. So, we can write:\n\n\\[ (t - 9)(t - 3) = 0 \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[ t - 9 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t - 3 = 0 \\]\n\n\\[ t = 9 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t = 3 \\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value is \\( t = 9 \\). To confirm, we can substitute \\( t = 9 \\) back into the original temperature equation:\n\n\\[ -9^2 + 12 \\cdot 9 + 50 = -81 + 108 + 50 \\]\n\n\\[ = 77 \\]\n\nThe temperature is indeed 77 degrees at \\( t = 9 \\). Finally, we find \\( \\boxed{9} \\).",
